{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Kda7VYhJEc5KbuvM7F4y7M","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"One test failed: `flag-hash-hex-zero`. The issue is that Go's `%#x` for value 0 produces `0x0`, but GNU printf (gprintf) produces `0`. This is because GNU printf follows the C standard where `#` with `%x` only adds the `0x` prefix for non-zero values.\n\nI need to fix the `fmtUnsigned` function to handle the `#` flag correctly for zero values with hex format. Actually, looking more carefully, the issue is that Go's `fmt.Fprintf` with `%#x` and value 0 produces `0x0`, while GNU's printf produces `0`.\n\n\nThe cleanest approach is to check in `fmtUnsigned` whether the value is 0 and the `#` flag is set, then strip the prefix accordingly to match GNU's behavior.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3191,"cache_read_input_tokens":33502,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3191},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4aa64e01-3c49-4eb7-afb6-9a9c876f853e","uuid":"a26c1ff7-c512-4200-aa14-957a437144f7"}
